# Cloudflare Pages response headers.
#
# NOTE: these rules currently change nothing. Pages already serves every asset
# as `public, max-age=0, must-revalidate`, so this only restates the default.
# Verified by comparing a listed file against an unlisted one on
# waybound.pages.dev — both identical.
#
# The 4-hour cache people actually hit comes from the ZONE, not from Pages:
# Cloudflare rewrites Cache-Control at the edge for static extensions (.css,
# .js) according to Caching -> Configuration -> Browser Cache TTL. HTML passes
# through untouched, which is why only assets go stale. No _headers rule can
# override that, because the rewrite happens downstream of the origin.
#
#   kavkazland.com/mobile.css      -> max-age=14400   (zone rewrote it)
#   waybound.pages.dev/mobile.css  -> max-age=0       (what Pages sent)
#
# Fix is one dropdown: Browser Cache TTL -> "Respect Existing Headers".
# Until that is set, assume any change to the files below takes up to 4 hours
# to reach a returning visitor. It matters most for config.js, which carries
# the API hostname — a stale copy points the browser at the old backend and
# fails before the request leaves the machine.
#
# These stay as documentation of intent, and become load-bearing the moment
# Pages' default changes or a rule below diverges from it.
/config.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/nav.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/toast.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate

/mobile.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate